 "brief": "The shoulder riser on my GRIP desk arm is a solid 60 x 50 x 40 mm block of MJF PA12 and weighs\n110 g, which is a lot to spin on the turntable. Origin at the centre of its bottom face, z up: it\noccupies x = -30 to 30, y = -25 to 25, z = 0 to 40, and sits flat on the turntable. Four M4 screws\ngo through it top to bottom into the turntable at (+/-22, +/-17); their heads sit in 8 mm\ncounterbores from z = 34 up, on solid seats. The shoulder bracket screws down onto the top with two\nM5 heat-set inserts at (+/-18, 0) (6.4 mm holes from z = 30 to the top), and a 16 mm cable hole runs\nthrough the middle along Z. The arm puts up to 20 N\u00b7m on the top through the inserts, about X or\nabout Y depending on where it points, plus 60 N down. Safety factor 2.5. Keep the outside exactly as\nit is (other parts locate on its faces) and keep the top 2 mm solid for the bracket to seat on, but\nfill the inside with a lattice that is denser where the stress is, with open cells and no closed\nskin so the powder can be blown out. Get it under 70 g.",
